[{:seon.task/name "a-bound-scalar-plans-like-a-literal"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by worktree session global-ids (2026-10-23, notes/global-ids/MEASURED.md §4). On a plain db (planner-eligible), a query whose value is bound through :in runs about 50x slower than the same query with the value literal: on a 115k-datom branch (seon.db/open! seed 50,000, 20 runs warm, then 200 timed, node cache 200,000 nodes), `[:find ?src ?o :in $ ?n :where [?v :var/name ?n] [?v :var/form ?f] [?f :form/src ?src] [?f :form/order ?o]]` with ?n 'app.m33/f7 took 8,062 us a query, the literal `[?v :var/name app.m33/f7] [?v :var/form ?f] [?f :form/src ?src]` 162 us, and one namespace's forms by `:in ?n` 7,398 us. The same bound queries over (d/history db) took 291 and 817 us, so the history path plans the bound scalar as a seek and the current path does not. Reproduction: notes/global-ids/bench/mk.clj (the store), then bench/m4.clj. Likely cause, unconfirmed: datahike.query's planner estimates a clause with an :in-bound scalar as an unbound scan (no AVET seek on :var/name, a unique identity), on the path a current db takes. Correct: a scalar bound through :in is a constant to the planner, so the bound query plans the AVET seek the literal one does, within 2x of it. Fixed in our Datahike fork on a jolt/<topic> branch at the planner's cause, with a cost proof at two sizes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-bound-scalar-plans-like-a-literal}}]
